  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nNot much to speak on at least in the very near-term. Current\nsatellite showing a band of scattered upper-level cirrostratus\nalong with some patches of mid-level cumulus across portions of\nnorthern down into western MN. Earlier today, this area of \ncumulus has been trying to squeeze out a few droplets of precip \nhowever, they are running into dry air as noted in our 12z UA \nsounding. Perhaps some of you have noticed that there is a stark\ncontrast between the hue of blue sky (or lack thereof) on the \nhorizon as compared to yesterday at this time. This is a result \nof from upper-level flow carrying in PacNorthwest wildfire \nsmoke. Skies will have a bit of haziness for at least the next \n24 to 36 hours before a cold front will push everything off to \nthe southeast. Temperatures for the remainder of today will \nreach near 80 degrees and perhaps may need to trend back a \ndegree or two based off of some elevated smoke limiting \"some\" \nsolar radiation's ability to heat the surface. It will feel a \nbit sticker too as compared to yesterday, dew points have crept \nback into the lower 60s as seen on western MN surface \nobservations. \n\nDew points will continue to trend up tonight into tomorrow\nthanks to a warm advective regime out ahead of a parent \ntroughing pattern which will span from Manitoba to Quebec. This \ntrough features a surface cold front that is expected to sweep \nthrough the northern plains from NW to SE through the course of \nFriday. There has been some guidance that wants to produce \nprecip for southwestern MN during the pre-dawn hours of Friday. \nHowever, digging into forecast soundings, enough surface dry air\nappears to be in place which should suppress all if any \nmoisture from reaching the surface which seems reasonably \nrepresented by the latest NBM PoPs. The concern for strong \nstorms starts late morning to early afternoon across eastern MN \ninto western WI. It is expect for CI to begin mainly near the \nI-35 corridor as early as 11AM although more likely near 2-3PM. \nMixed layer instability nearing 2000 J/kg, combined with steep \nlapse rates between 7-8km supports a few stronger updrafts could\ndevelop within a broken line of storms. Thus the SPC has \nmaintained their marginal risk for severe storms mainly for \nareas east of line from New Ulm, MN, up to the Twin Cities \nMetro, then up through Taylors Falls, WI. Primary threats will \nmainly be an isolated damaging wind gust up to 60 mph and large \nhail around 1\" diameter. Rainfall amounts wont be too exciting \nwith areas seeing a few hundredths to a couple tenths of a inch \nwith locally higher amounts limited to any stronger cells. \n\nAfter the cold front shoves off to our south and east Friday\nnight, we will receive a short break from the sticker \nconditions on Saturday. At least for the first half of the day, \ndew points fall back into the 50s with temperatures in the low \nto mid 80s for forecasted highs. The aforementioned troughing \npattern will remain quasi-stationary over the Hudson Bay. This \nwill transition our upper- flow to more zonal in nature allowing\nfor of an active weather pattern for the weekend. The next shot\nof rain chances comes Saturday night as a weak wave provides a \nglancing blow mainly along the I-90 corridor. The following wave\ncoming in from the Dakotas looks to have better localized \nforcing for us thus better chances of seeing rain/storms Sunday \nnight into Monday.",
